# OAuth2 client credentials

Generated by `ddd recipe oauth2-client-credentials --dry-run`, then the same
command without `--dry-run`. This adds a dependency-free grant client, Nest
sync/async module, tests, and the exact bounded JSON HTTP primitive used by the
introspection recipe. Both recipes refuse existing changed output; do not delete
adapted files to force an upgrade. No AppModule registration or permission grant.

`OAuth2ClientCredentialsClient` takes a fixed HTTPS `tokenUrl`, `clientId`,
`clientSecret`, optional `scopes`, and bounded timeout/response/token limits.
It uses RFC6749 client-secret Basic authentication (form-encoded components),
`grant_type=client_credentials`, and requires Bearer type plus explicit bounded
positive `expires_in`. Decimal strings are rejected unless the consumer explicitly
sets `allowStringExpiresIn: true` for a documented provider contract.

`getAccessToken()` caches until a conservative expiry calculated from request
start; `renewalMarginMs` is bounded and capped at ten percent of token lifetime.
Concurrent callers share one grant acquisition. Failure clears that acquisition;
no stale token, background grant, refresh-token path or implicit retry exists.
`invalidate(rejectedToken)` evicts only that current token. It does not resend a
provider operation; definite-rejection classification belongs to the provider.

All credential/cache storage uses ECMAScript private fields. Errors retain only
safe reason and optional HTTP status, without raw responses, causes or headers.
Configuration options and returned tokens remain secret-bearing: never log them.

The bounded helper supports `get`, `postForm`, and `postJson`. JSON is snapshotted
before awaiting, supports bounded plain values, and refuses cycles, accessors,
non-finite numbers, unsupported prototypes, excessive depth/nodes/UTF8 bytes.
Its trusted headers are immutable and hidden from object serialization. Construct
a fresh helper with the current Bearer token after acquisition rather than mutate
the original header object. HTTP has no redirects, no implicit retries, bounded
response/deadline and sanitized errors. Providers own response schema/acceptance.

This is transport source infrastructure. It does not prove provider credentials,
funding, destination/sender capability, delivered messages, authorization or an
exactly-once external side effect. Unknown provider write results require owning
reconciliation and cannot be retried by this helper.

Protocol: [RFC6749 client credentials](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749#section-4.4).
